WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.600
This is a Wendover Productions video made
possible by Squarespace.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.520
Make your next move with a beautiful website
from Squarespace.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:13.380
The Cumberland valley is home to six towns
lying between Hagerstown, Maryland and Harrisburg,

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.480
Pennsylvania— Greencastle, Chambersburg,
Shippensburg, Newville, Carlisle, and Mecanicsburg.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:24.910
What’s exceptional about these small Pennsylvania
towns is that they’re each almost exactly

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:26.319
10 miles from each other.

00:00:26.319 --> 00:00:30.649
The distances deviate by no more than a mile
from this rule.

00:00:30.649 --> 00:00:33.540
This isn’t a coincidence and this isn’t
planned.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:38.980
Drawing equal sized radii around each town
shows you their spheres of influence.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.330
Assuming each town has the exact same shops
and services, rational people will just go

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:47.220
to whichever town is closest to buy or sell
goods.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:52.030
Towns ten miles apart mean that nobody has
to travel more than five miles to reach a

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:53.030
town.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:56.620
Each one of these towns was founded before
the formation of the United States, so that

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.510
means that, of course, nobody had cars and
pretty much everybody walked everywhere.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:07.280
10 miles, or 5 miles each way, is about the
distance a person can comfortably walk in

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.600
a day with enough time to buy or sell goods
at a central market.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.630
Back in this era before cars, a 5 mile radius
was essentially the largest possible commuter

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:22.340
zone to small agricultural towns and therefore
having towns ten miles apart was the most

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.270
efficient possible use of rural land.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:30.030
When you get a chance, take a look at map
of a rural area that existed before cars.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:35.250
You’ll see that the distance between medium-sized
towns is almost always somewhere between about

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.620
10 to 15 miles.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:42.280
Because the Cumberland valley is a valley,
towns really could only develop in a line,

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.690
but in most cases towns develop in all directions.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.360
This is what the ten mile rule looks like
going out in all directions.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.760
Each of these points is a town and the hexagon
around it is the area from which people will

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.180
go to the town.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:00.310
In the real world, each of these towns probably
has a small grocery store, a pharmacy, a bank,

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:01.680
and maybe a restaurant.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.240
Since everybody uses these services, there
doesn’t have to be many people in a towns

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.780
sphere of influence in order to sustain these
shops.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.370
But where do you put something more specialized,
like a mechanic.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:17.180
People only need to go the mechanic every
once in a while so you need more people to

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:20.420
sustain one mechanics shop than one grocery
store.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:25.120
Well, some of these small towns develop into
larger towns with more people that can support

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.000
more specialized shops and services.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.590
Putting these larger towns with more specialized
shops closer together would be unsustainable

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:36.420
since there wouldn’t be enough people going
to those shops but putting them farther apart

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.670
would be inefficient since there’s land
that people would not go to a city from.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.970
This happens once or twice more until you
have cities.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:48.540
These cities have the largest spheres of influence
and the most specialized shops.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.730
You of course still have grocery stores and
pharmacies in cities, but you also have things

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:56.500
like luxury car dealerships, brain surgery
centers, and airports.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:02.290
The city’s sphere of influence is enormous
because people will travel hundreds of miles

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:06.920
to buy an expensive car or get brain surgery
or fly from an airport.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.420
Think about it within a city.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.550
How far would you walk to buy a latte.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.879
Probably only a few blocks and that’s why
you see Starbucks or other coffee shops on

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:17.580
almost every block.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:22.250
Since almost everyone buys coffee, you only
need a few blocks of people to sustain one

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:23.250
coffee shop.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.170
But how far would you walk to buy a MacBook?

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:29.989
Probably quite far since its a infrequent
and substantial purchase.

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:33.820
That’s why Apple stores are rather rare
even in cities.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:38.980
You need an enormous amount of people to sustain
one Apple store and we can actually figure

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.650
out roughly how many.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:45.230
In Connecticut, the Trumbull Apple Store is
about 20 miles away from the New Haven store

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:48.220
to the north-east and the Stamford store to
the south-west.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.970
In the 10 mile radius around the Trumbull
Apple Store there are about half a million

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:57.700
inhabitants which tells us that you need about
half a million people to sustain one Apple

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:58.700
store.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:02.390
We can compare that to the Starbucks’ of
lower Manhattan which are spread out at an

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:05.150
average distance of about 600 feet.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:11.560
Drawing a 300 foot radius around one Starbucks
in lower Manhattan covers around 6,000 people

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:15.730
which means that one Starbucks needs 6,000
people to sustain it.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.979
Of course both Connecticut and New York are
places with higher than average incomes which

00:04:19.979 --> 00:04:23.900
means less people are needed to sustain one
Starbucks or Apple Store.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:28.470
The numbers would be very different in, say,
rural Kansas, but since each store generally

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:33.010
only builds in areas with higher-than-average
incomes this gives a good sense of how many

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:37.630
people Apple and Starbucks looks for in an
area before opening up a store.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:43.199
So, our model shows where cities should be,
but its not like this in reality.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:47.051
This is the most efficient spread of cities
if you’re assuming that the cities are on

00:04:47.051 --> 00:04:53.270
a perfectly flat plane with no geographic
features, no social influences, no variability

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:59.760
of income, equal distribution of resources—essentially
assuming the world is one homogeneous place…

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:00.760
which its not.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:06.750
In reality, of course, our world has an enormous
effect on where and why cities develop.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:11.479
To start out, let’s cut this down to one
city on a flat, featureless plane for simplicity.

00:05:11.479 --> 00:05:15.400
What affects the location of cities more than
anything is water.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:20.410
If we put an ocean on one side of our isotropic
plane, our city will almost certainly locate

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:21.590
near it.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:25.010
Oceans have always been and still are what
connects the world.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:29.630
There’s no other means of transport that
can move such enormous amounts of cargo for

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:30.919
so little.

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:35.830
Any city needs to be economically efficient
to grow and it will cost more to bring goods

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:40.610
to a city that’s 1000 miles inland than
one right by the ocean.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:41.610
Just look at Europe.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:46.280
6 of the 10 largest European cities are within
100 miles of the coast.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.650
But oceans aren’t the only bodies of water
to affect cities.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:52.940
Rivers are just as or perhaps even more influential.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:58.531
Milan, the 19th largest European city, is
the largest to not be either directly on the

00:05:58.531 --> 00:06:04.270
ocean or on a river, and even then its only
15 miles from a river and 75 miles from the

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:05.280
ocean.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.330
Until the last century or so, cities could
not survive without direct water access.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:15.540
If you need more proof, 14 of the 15 largest
cities in the world are within a few dozen

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.370
miles of the ocean.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:22.120
Perhaps the most obvious attractor for cities
is resources, so going back to our isotropic

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:27.020
plane, putting natural resources anywhere
on this map will draw cities near it.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:32.110
Cities that existed before the last century
or so generally sprung up right near the resources,

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:36.370
much like Pittsburgh, since they acted as
manufacturing and transportation hubs for

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:41.800
those resources, but more recently new resource
dependent cities don’t need to be as close

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.720
to the resources themselves.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:48.020
New transportation technologies can bring
the resources from their source.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.300
Just look at Dubai.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:53.990
Of course the UAE has enormous oil deposits,
but they’re much closer to Abu Dhabi and

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:56.630
the South-West than Dubai.

00:06:56.630 --> 00:07:02.590
In 1900, Dubai had 10,000 residents, less
than half that of Carlisle, Pennsylvania—one

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:05.300
of the farming towns we talked about at the
beginning.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:12.880
That only grew to 40,000 by 1960, but today
its known worldwide and has more than 2.5

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:13.880
million residents.

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:19.180
It was able to grow at this enormous rate—even
faster than Abu Dhabi—since it cemented

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:24.380
itself as the economic and administrative
hub for the oil industries of the region.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:27.860
Another geographic feature that we can add
to the plane is mountains.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:32.210
Now, mountains don’t always have a uniform
affect on cities.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:39.180
Mexico City, Bogota, and Addis Ababa are all
enormous cities at elevations above 7,000

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:40.180
feet.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:44.870
Mountains do make transport and trade difficult,
but they also provide protection.

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:49.400
Many ancient cities grew in these locations
since they were easy to protect, which left

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:54.669
more time to focus on growing the city, but
mountains can also hinder development.

00:07:54.669 --> 00:07:59.259
For quite a while, the United States could
not develop west of the Appalachian mountains.

00:07:59.259 --> 00:08:01.710
They just served as an enormous barrier.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:08.110
In 1800, the average center of population
for the entire United States was here even

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:11.740
though the US had sovereignty over this entire
area.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:15.860
Of course technology eventually conquered
this barrier and moved the mean population

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:20.590
center all the way out to Missouri today,
but if the Appalachian mountains didn’t

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:24.770
exist American history and geography would
be completely different.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.580
We would have seen urban development much
earlier in the mid-west.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:30.600
But mountains can have another effect.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:36.079
You see, coal, silver, gold, and other mineral
deposits are all often located in mountainous

00:08:36.079 --> 00:08:41.189
regions, and, just like Dubai, cities can
develop in less hospitable and easy places

00:08:41.189 --> 00:08:42.699
due to resources.

00:08:42.699 --> 00:08:48.040
The economic advantage of exploiting the resources
overpowers the economic disadvantage of being

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:50.239
in an inhospitable location.

00:08:50.239 --> 00:08:57.360
Denver, Colorado grew 650% between 1870 and
1880 with the opening of a railroad branch

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:59.959
connecting with the transcontinental railroad.

00:08:59.959 --> 00:09:04.819
It served as an access point to transportation
to the gold miners in the rockies.

00:09:04.819 --> 00:09:09.370
So mountains can either push cities away or
bring them nearer—it really just depends

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:10.869
on the circumstance.

00:09:10.869 --> 00:09:14.110
Let’s exchange our isotropic plane for a
world map.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:15.730
Where should cities be on here?

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:22.089
Well, our world’s cities are not necessarily
all in the most geographically efficient locations.

00:09:22.089 --> 00:09:26.410
While there is a certain level of natural
selection that grows the efficiently placed

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:30.800
cities and shrinks the inefficiently placed
cities, humans are not always able to put

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.170
cities in the most efficient locations.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:38.569
Let’s put up the 224 cities in the world
with a population over 2 million.

00:09:38.569 --> 00:09:40.860
You can immediately see some patterns.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:44.160
Putting up the equator, you can see a clear
divide.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.949
Only 32 of these cities lie in the southern
hemisphere.

00:09:47.949 --> 00:09:52.589
One might think this is because there is so
much more land in the northern hemisphere,

00:09:52.589 --> 00:09:54.439
but that’s not entirely true.

00:09:54.439 --> 00:10:00.689
You see, the southern hemisphere still has
32% of the world’s land, but only has 14%

00:10:00.689 --> 00:10:02.360
of the world’s large cities.

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:06.589
There’s clearly a higher density of cities
in the northern hemisphere.

00:10:06.589 --> 00:10:09.749
You can pretty much trace this all back to
Europe and Asia.

00:10:09.749 --> 00:10:14.410
The first large civilizations and empires
were on these two continents even though the

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:17.649
human race likely originated in Africa.

00:10:17.649 --> 00:10:21.779
There’s hundreds of different theories on
why civilizations succeeded in some places

00:10:21.779 --> 00:10:25.929
and failed in others, but one of the more
plausible and interesting theories is that

00:10:25.929 --> 00:10:29.850
Europe and Asia succeeded because they’re
wide instead of tall.

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:33.959
The very shape of the continents may have
changed the course of human history.

00:10:33.959 --> 00:10:39.959
You see, when a continent is wide, you have
a ton of land with roughly the same climate.

00:10:39.959 --> 00:10:44.040
Climate tends to change when you go north
and south rather than east and west as a nature

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.610
of how the earth rotates around the sun.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:50.989
Much of the success of early civilizations
had to do with the domestication of plants

00:10:50.989 --> 00:10:53.629
and animals and the corresponding technology.

00:10:53.629 --> 00:10:57.739
When expanding horizontally, the climate is
similar enough that an empire can use the

00:10:57.739 --> 00:11:02.829
same successful plants and animals, while
expanding vertically requires the domestication

00:11:02.829 --> 00:11:04.920
of new plants and animals.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.709
If a civilization started in central-america,
for example, there would be very little land

00:11:09.709 --> 00:11:14.839
on the continent with a similar climate and
their expansion would be severely limited.

00:11:14.839 --> 00:11:20.220
In Europe and Asia, on the other hand, theres
thousands upon thousands and miles of similar

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:23.429
climate that can be reached just by traveling
east or west.

00:11:23.429 --> 00:11:24.429
There’s evidence to back this up.

00:11:24.429 --> 00:11:29.389
Just look at the maps of the four largest
early empires—the Qing Dynasty, the Abbasid

00:11:29.389 --> 00:11:32.940
Caliphate, the Umayyad Caliphate, and the
Mongol empire.

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:36.980
They were all in Eurasia and they all expanded
horizontally.

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:42.119
When some of the more modern empires expanded,
they had the technology to do so overseas.

00:11:42.119 --> 00:11:47.420
The three major modern empires were the British,
Spanish, and French empires—each of which

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:49.610
came from relatively similar climates.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:55.220
A major reason why America was able to succeed
is because all the agriculture from Europe

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:56.759
worked there.

00:11:56.759 --> 00:12:00.370
Climatically, Europe and America are nearly
identical.

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:04.619
The majority of developed colonized countries
are in the northern hemisphere just because

00:12:04.619 --> 00:12:09.649
they were closest to Europe, but formerly
British countries like South Africa, Australia,

00:12:09.649 --> 00:12:13.410
and New Zealand are all highly developed and
in the Southern Hemisphere.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:17.859
Their success over more northern countries
in the southern hemisphere can also be partially

00:12:17.859 --> 00:12:20.989
attributed to their greater climate similarity
to Europe.

00:12:20.989 --> 00:12:23.529
Let’s ask one more question.

00:12:23.529 --> 00:12:28.059
If our world only had one city, where would
it logically be?

00:12:28.059 --> 00:12:32.959
Well if you take the location of every person
in the world and average it out, you come

00:12:32.959 --> 00:12:34.730
to south-central Asia.

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:40.449
That means that this general region is the
optimum place to live on the planet, but where

00:12:40.449 --> 00:12:43.899
more specifically should our world city go.

00:12:43.899 --> 00:12:48.910
Well, this region is already in the Northern
Hemisphere and in Eurasia, so we’ve already

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.129
covered those two criteria.

00:12:51.129 --> 00:12:56.769
We want a place within a hundred of so miles
of the ocean, on a navigable river, near mountains

00:12:56.769 --> 00:13:02.600
with rich mineral deposits—the single best
place for a city on earth just might be…

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:04.480
Dhaka, Bangladesh.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:10.149
Every geographic model and theory says that
there is no better place on earth to put a

00:13:10.149 --> 00:13:11.249
city than here.

00:13:11.249 --> 00:13:16.259
There’s evidence to back this up: Dhaka
is between the 4th and 18th largest metropolitan

00:13:16.259 --> 00:13:21.869
area on earth depending on how you define
metropolitan area, and Bangladesh is the sixth

00:13:21.869 --> 00:13:29.660
densest country on earth—there are 161 million
people living in an area about the size of

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:30.660
England.

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:34.809
History has affected geography enough that
the largest and most advanced civilizations

00:13:34.809 --> 00:13:39.990
are not all in South-Central Asia, but if
we started all over again, did humanity a

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:46.410
second time, every geographic model says that
this region could be the origin and central

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:48.660
point of human civilization.

